# Docker deployment only. Copy to .env, beside docker-compose.yml.
#
# This file is read by `docker compose` itself, to fill in the ${...} values in
# docker-compose.yml -- it is NOT the application's .env. The server's own
# settings are in .env.server.example, and inside a container they come from the
# compose file's `environment:` block instead.
#
# Everything here is optional. An empty file, or no file at all, is a working
# deployment.

# Who the server runs as inside the container, and who ./data ends up belonging
# to. docker-compose.yml already defaults these to 1030 and 100, the docker user
# on the Synology this is deployed to, so on that box there is nothing to set.
#
# On any other machine, put its own ids here. `id <your-user>` prints both.
# PUID=1000
# PGID=1000
#
# Set either to empty and the container adopts whoever already owns ./data
# instead, which works without knowing any of this.
# PUID=
# PGID=

# Password for the library. Empty disables the login entirely, which is often
# what you want at home and never what you want if this is reachable from
# outside your network.
# MIDI_MEMORY_PASSWORD=
